title: Read an Article
category: activity
status: draft
order: 2
icon: file-text
summary: Design accessible, focused and purposeful reading materials that support comprehension, active reading and continued learning.
---

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="content" %}
# Content design

## The article’s structure is aligned with the intended learning results

Define the intended learning results before drafting the article. Use them to determine its scope, sequence and emphasis, and map each major section, explanation, example, illustration and learning prompt to the knowledge or performance learners are expected to develop.[^learning-objectives][^constructive-alignment]

Include the content and evidence needed to support those outcomes, and remove material that has no clear explanatory, evidential, contextual or motivational purpose. For exploratory or interpretive reading, organise the article around a guiding question or enquiry purpose rather than forcing a single predetermined conclusion. Do not treat the mere presence of a learning-outcomes statement as evidence that the article itself is aligned.[^instructional-text-objectives]

[^learning-objectives]: [Writing and using learning objectives](https://doi.org/10.1187/cbe.22-04-0073 "Orr, R. B., Csikari, M. M., Freeman, S., & Rodriguez, M. C. (2022). Writing and Using Learning Objectives. CBE—Life Sciences Education, 21(3), fe3. This article provides evidence-based guidance for writing and using learning objectives, supporting the recommendation to state what learners should achieve.")

[^constructive-alignment]: [Enhancing teaching through constructive alignment](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00138871 "Biggs, J. (1996). Enhancing Teaching through Constructive Alignment. Higher Education, 32(3), 347–364. This article introduces constructive alignment, supporting the alignment of learning outcomes, activities and assessment.")

[^instructional-text-objectives]: [Intentional and incidental learning with instructional texts](https://doi.org/10.3102/00028312021002323 "Klauer, K. J. (1984). Intentional and Incidental Learning with Instructional Texts: A Meta-analysis for 1970–1980. American Educational Research Journal, 21(2), 323–339. The review found that objectives and learning directions improved learning of goal-relevant material but could reduce learning of material outside the stated goals.")

## The article has a clear and relevant title

Use a concise title that identifies the article’s topic or purpose and remains meaningful when shown outside the article, such as in a course outline, search result or list of learning activities. Where appropriate, connect the topic to a problem, question, decision, outcome or real-world situation that matters to the intended learners.[^descriptive-title][^reading-motivation]

The title may create curiosity, but it should provide enough information for learners to judge the article’s relevance. Avoid generic labels, unfamiliar jargon, exaggerated promises and clickbait that conceals the actual subject. Ensure that the title accurately represents the article’s content and intended learning results.[^headline-concreteness]

[^descriptive-title]: [Providing descriptive titles for web pages](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G88 "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique G88: Providing Descriptive Titles for Web Pages. This technique supports titles that identify the subject, make sense out of context and remain concise.")

[^reading-motivation]: [Effectiveness of interventions that foster reading motivation](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-023-09719-3 "van der Sande, L., van Steensel, R., Fikrat-Wevers, S., & Arends, L. (2023). Effectiveness of Interventions that Foster Reading Motivation: A Meta-analysis. Educational Psychology Review, 35, Article 21. The review identifies triggering interest and connecting reading to meaningful contexts among strategies that can support reading motivation.")

[^headline-concreteness]: [When curiosity gaps backfire](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-81575-9 "Aubin Le Quéré, M., & Matias, J. N. (2025). When Curiosity Gaps Backfire: Effects of Headline Concreteness on Information Selection Decisions. Scientific Reports, 15, Article 994. The study found that both insufficiently informative and overly concrete headlines can reduce selection, suggesting that titles should balance clarity with curiosity. This evidence concerns news headlines rather than learning articles.")


## A lead paragraph introduces the article and addresses the learners

Open with a concise paragraph that explains what the article is about, why it is relevant to the intended learners and how it relates to the current learning context. Signal the central question, problem or argument, and mention any prior knowledge needed to understand the article. This orientation can activate relevant knowledge and provide a framework for interpreting what follows.[^advance-organisers]

When the same article is reused across different courses or activities, tailor the lead paragraph to emphasise its particular purpose and value in each context. Use language and examples appropriate to the learners. Do not merely repeat the title, list the learning results or summarise the entire article; give learners enough context and reason to begin reading without removing the need to engage with the article itself.[^reading-motivation]

[^advance-organisers]: [A meta-analysis of the effects of advance organizers on learning and retention](https://doi.org/10.3102/00028312017002211 "Luiten, J., Ames, W., & Ackerson, G. (1980). A Meta-analysis of the Effects of Advance Organizers on Learning and Retention. American Educational Research Journal, 17(2), 211–218. The review found an overall positive effect of advance organisers on learning and retention, while effects varied by design and learner characteristics.")


## The article takes about 5–10 minutes to read

Use 5–10 minutes as a design target for a focused article within an online learning activity. There is no universally established optimal duration for reading learning content, but this range is consistent with durations commonly used in published microlearning studies.[^microlearning-duration]

Estimate the initial duration using approximately 200 words per minute for proficient readers reading in their native language. For learners reading in a non-native language, reduce the planning rate by approximately 25–50%, to about 100–150 words per minute, depending on their proficiency and the complexity and familiarity of the content. Where possible, verify the estimate with representative learners rather than relying on word count alone.[^reading-rate][^second-language-reading-rate]

Treat the total reading activity time as the learner’s total time for engaging with the article, not only the time required to read its words. Allow time for understanding unfamiliar concepts, interpreting examples and illustrations, reflecting on the content and briefly revisiting important passages.

[^microlearning-duration]: [Microlearning beyond boundaries](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e41413 "Monib, W. K., Qazi, A., & Apong, R. A. (2025). Microlearning beyond boundaries: A systematic review and a novel framework for improving learning outcomes. Heliyon, 11(2), e41413. The review reports that no agreed ideal duration exists and identifies durations ranging from 1–3 to 10–15 minutes across included studies.")

[^reading-rate]: [How many words do we read per minute?](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jml.2019.104047 "Brysbaert, M. (2019). How many words do we read per minute? A review and meta-analysis of reading rate. Journal of Memory and Language, 109, 104047. The study estimates an average silent reading rate of 238 words per minute for English non-fiction and reports lower rates for second-language readers.")

[^second-language-reading-rate]: [Tracking reading development in an English-language university-level bridging program](https://doi.org/10.1017/S1366728922000542 "Schmidtke, D., Rahmanian, S., & Moro, A. L. (2023). Tracking reading development in an English-language university-level bridging program: Evidence from eye movements during passage reading. Bilingualism: Language and Cognition, 26(2), 356–370. Students reached an estimated 103–130 words per minute, averaging approximately 116 words per minute compared with the 238-word native-English benchmark.")


## Longer articles are divided into sections with table of contents

Divide longer or multi-topic articles into meaningful sections or chapters with concise, descriptive headings. Align section boundaries with conceptual transitions and the article’s argument rather than arbitrary lengths, and use a logical heading hierarchy without skipping levels. Clear text structure helps learners understand how ideas relate, locate relevant information and revisit specific explanations.[^text-structure][^heading-structure]

Add an in-page table of contents when the article contains several independently useful sections or requires substantial scrolling. Use the same descriptive headings in the contents list and link each entry directly to its section. Keep the list concise and avoid adding it to short articles where it would introduce more complexity than navigation value.[^long-content-navigation]

[^text-structure]: [The effects of text structure instruction on expository reading comprehension](https://doi.org/10.1037/edu0000082 "Hebert, M., Bohaty, J. J., Nelson, J. R., & Brown, J. (2016). The Effects of Text Structure Instruction on Expository Reading Comprehension: A Meta-analysis. Journal of Educational Psychology, 108(5), 609–629. This meta-analysis supports using explicit text structures to improve expository reading comprehension.")

[^heading-structure]: [Headings](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/page-structure/headings/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2017). Page Structure Tutorial: Headings. This tutorial supports headings that communicate content organisation, provide in-page navigation and follow a logical hierarchy.")

[^long-content-navigation]: [Structure in content author modules](https://www.w3.org/WAI/curricula/content-author-modules/structure/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2022). Structure in Content Author Modules. This guidance supports meaningful heading hierarchies and methods for navigating long content, including tables of contents.")

## Illustrations support and clarify the reading material

Use illustrations when they explain relationships, structure, sequence, comparison, evidence or context that learners need to understand. Choose diagrams, charts, screenshots, photographs or other visuals according to their instructional purpose, and keep them accurate, legible and aligned with the surrounding text and intended learning outcomes.[^multimedia-principle]

Place each illustration close to the passage it supports. Use a concise caption and refer to the illustration explicitly in the text so learners know what to notice. Remove decorative or irrelevant details that compete with the explanation.[^coherence-principle]

[^multimedia-principle]: [The multimedia principle](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.015 "Mayer, R. E. (2021). The Multimedia Principle. In R. E. Mayer & L. Fiorella (Eds.), The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press. This chapter supports combining words with relevant visuals when they help learners understand the material.")

[^coherence-principle]: [Principles for reducing extraneous processing in multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Fiorella, L., & Mayer, R. E. (2021). Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing in Multimedia Learning: Coherence, Signaling, Redundancy, Spatial Contiguity, and Temporal Contiguity Principles. In R. E. Mayer & L. Fiorella (Eds.), The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press. This chapter supports removing decorative or irrelevant details that would compete with the explanation.")


## Special terms are highlighted, explained and linked to the glossary

Identify terms, abbreviations and concepts that may be unfamiliar to the intended learners. Explain each essential term in plain language when it is first introduced, using an example where this helps learners understand how the term is applied. Introducing the names and defining characteristics of key concepts before they are used in a complex explanation can reduce unnecessary processing demands.[^pretraining]

Make [glossary][browse-a-glossary] terms visually distinguishable without relying on colour alone, and link their first meaningful occurrence to the corresponding glossary entry. Use the same term and definition consistently throughout the article and related learning materials. The glossary should provide additional reference support, not replace the contextual explanation needed to understand the current passage.[^unusual-words][^glossary]

[^pretraining]: [The pre-training principle](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139547369.016 "Mayer, R. E., & Pilegard, C. (2014). Principles for Managing Essential Processing in Multimedia Learning: Segmenting, Pre-training, and Modality Principles. In The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 2nd ed. Cambridge University Press. This chapter supports introducing key terms and their characteristics before learners encounter complex explanations.")

[^unusual-words]: [Understanding unusual words](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/unusual-words.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 3.1.3: Unusual Words. This guidance supports providing definitions for technical jargon and words used in an unusual or restricted way.")

[^glossary]: [Providing a glossary](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G62.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique G62: Providing a Glossary. This technique supports making definitions of discipline-specific words, phrases and abbreviations available to readers.")


## Key conclusions and takeaways are provided

Conclude the article with a concise summary of its central ideas, findings, implications or recommended actions. Ensure that every takeaway is supported by the article, aligned with its intended learning outcomes and specific enough to help learners review what matters most.[^main-idea-instruction]

Keep the summary brief and do not introduce new information or reproduce the article section by section. Where appropriate, ask learners to recall, select or formulate the main points before revealing the provided takeaways, so the summary supports active processing rather than becoming a substitute for reading.

[^main-idea-instruction]: [Review of summarising and main-idea interventions](https://doi.org/10.1177/0741932517749940 "Stevens, E. A., Park, S., & Vaughn, S. (2019). A Review of Summarizing and Main Idea Interventions for Struggling Readers in Grades 3 Through 12: 1978–2016. Remedial and Special Education, 40(3), 131–149. This review supports concise summaries that help learners identify an article’s main ideas.")

## References and sources are declared

Cite quotations, data, adapted ideas and claims that depend on external evidence at the passage they support. Make it clear which statements come from a source and which are the author’s interpretation, and distinguish primary evidence from reviews, commentary or secondary reporting where this affects how the information should be understood.[^information-literacy]

Provide a consistently formatted reference list with enough information to identify and retrieve each source, including its author or organisation, title, publication date and publication or publisher. Use descriptive source titles rather than bare URLs or generic link text, and include a DOI or another persistent identifier where available. Verify that every citation corresponds to the intended source and that every listed reference is used in the article.[^link-purpose][^doi-display]

[^information-literacy]: [Framework for Information Literacy for Higher Education](https://www.ala.org/acrl/standards/ilframework "Association of College and Research Libraries. (2015). Framework for Information Literacy for Higher Education. The framework addresses evaluating the authority, origin, context and suitability of information and giving credit to the original ideas of others.")

[^doi-display]: [DOI display guidelines](https://www.crossref.org/display-guidelines/ "Crossref. (n.d.). DOI Display Guidelines. This guidance supports presenting persistent DOI links in reference lists and other scholarly content.")

## Supplementary materials and further reading are clearly identified

Provide supplementary materials only when they extend, deepen, exemplify, update or support the application of the article’s content. Keep all information required to understand the article and achieve its intended learning outcomes within the article itself; learners should not need to follow optional links to access essential explanations or evidence.[^coherence-principle]

Place optional resources in a clearly labelled **Supplementary materials** or **Further reading** section, or beside the passage they directly support. For each resource, use a descriptive title and briefly explain what it contains and why it may be useful. Clearly distinguish optional resources from required activities, limit the selection to relevant and trustworthy materials, and avoid using **Read more** as the link text without a descriptive resource name.[^link-purpose]

[^link-purpose]: [Understanding link purpose](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/link-purpose-in-context.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.4: Link Purpose (In Context). This guidance supports link text and its programmatically determined context identifying the destination or purpose of a link.")


## Reflective and decision-making questions accompany the article

Provide a small set of focused questions immediately after the article or as a clearly linked follow-up activity. Use recall questions to help learners retrieve central ideas, reflective questions to connect the content with their prior knowledge or experience, and decision-making questions to apply it to a relevant choice, judgement or scenario.[^retrieval-practice][^self-explanation]

Align each question with the article’s intended learning outcomes and ask learners to explain their reasoning rather than select an answer without reflection. Keep prompts specific and relevant instead of relying on generic questions such as “What did you think?”. For questions with a correct or defensible answer, provide timely feedback and an opportunity to revisit the relevant part of the article.[^reflection-prompts]

[^retrieval-practice]: [Retrieval practice consistently benefits student learning](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-021-09595-9 "Agarwal, P. K., Nunes, L. D., & Blunt, J. R. (2021). Retrieval Practice Consistently Benefits Student Learning: A Systematic Review of Applied Research in Schools and Classrooms. Educational Psychology Review, 33, 1409–1453. This systematic review supports using retrieval prompts to strengthen learning from articles.")

[^self-explanation]: [Inducing self-explanation](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-018-9434-x "Bisra, K., Liu, Q., Nesbit, J. C., Salimi, F., & Winne, P. H. (2018). Inducing Self-Explanation: A Meta-analysis. Educational Psychology Review, 30(3), 703–725. This meta-analysis supports asking learners to explain material to themselves as they read.")

[^reflection-prompts]: [Effects of reflection prompts when learning with hypermedia](https://doi.org/10.2190/94V6-R58H-3367-G388 "Bannert, M. (2006). Effects of Reflection Prompts When Learning with Hypermedia. Journal of Educational Computing Research, 35(4), 359–375. This study supports reflection prompts that help learners monitor and connect ideas while reading.")


## Text alternatives are provided for embedded media

Provide a text alternative that communicates the purpose and essential information of every informative image, chart, diagram, animation or other non-text element. Adapt the alternative to the media’s function and context rather than describing every visible detail, and avoid repeating information already available in the surrounding text.[^non-text-content]

Provide a longer description or equivalent data representation when complex media cannot be explained adequately in a short alternative. Mark purely decorative media so assistive technologies can ignore it. For audio and video, provide the appropriate synchronized or descriptive alternatives, such as captions, transcripts and audio descriptions.[^image-alternatives][^time-based-media]

[^non-text-content]: [Understanding non-text content](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-content.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 1.1.1: Non-text Content. This guidance supports text alternatives for non-text content that serve an equivalent purpose.")

[^image-alternatives]: [Images tutorial](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Images Tutorial. This tutorial supports appropriate alternatives for informative, functional, decorative and complex images.")

[^time-based-media]: [Making audio and video media accessible](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2024). Making Audio and Video Media Accessible. This guidance supports captions, transcripts, audio descriptions and accessible media players.")

## Localized versions are available for target regions

Provide localized versions where language or regional context could limit understanding or relevance. Translate the complete article, including its title, lead paragraph, headings, illustrations, captions, questions, takeaways, glossary terms and other supporting content.[^multilingual-education]

Adapt terminology, examples, units, cultural references and visual content where necessary—not only the words. Have translations reviewed by proficient human reviewers, label each language and locale clearly, and keep all localized versions synchronized when the source article changes.[^content-language]

[^multilingual-education]: [Languages matter: global guidance on multilingual education](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/languages-matter-global-guidance-multilingual-education "UNESCO. (2025). Languages Matter: Global Guidance on Multilingual Education. This guidance supports designing learning materials with learners’ language backgrounds in mind.")

[^content-language]: [Understanding language of page](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/language-of-page.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 3.1.1: Language of Page. This guidance supports making the default human language of each page programmatically identifiable.")

{% /pattern-list %}

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="interactions" %}
# Interactions design

## The estimated reading time is stated

Display the estimated reading time before the article begins, close to its title and lead paragraph, so learners can assess the expected workload and plan when to read it. Present the estimate as a range where meaningful variation is expected, and state whether it covers only reading the text or the total time required to engage with illustrations, embedded media, reflection prompts, questions and other included activities.[^page-time-estimation]

Calculate the estimate from the article’s word count and the additional time expected for non-text content and learning activities. Adjust it for the complexity and familiarity of the material, the intended learners’ language proficiency and relevant accessibility needs. Prefer rates observed with representative learners where possible. Do not present the estimate as a deadline, use elapsed time as evidence of comprehension or completion, or penalise learners for taking longer.[^reading-rate][^workload-estimation]

[^page-time-estimation]: [Estimating time to process online learning pages](https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2017.1341941 "Bälter, O., & Zimmaro, D. (2018). Keystroke-level analysis to estimate time to process pages in online learning environments. Interactive Learning Environments, 26(4), 476–485. The study estimated time for text, figures and questions and recommends presenting estimates as intervals because individual processing times vary.")

[^workload-estimation]: [Estimating student workload in online courses](https://eprints.lancs.ac.uk/id/eprint/140685/ "Beer, N. (2019). Estimating Student Workload During the Learning Design of Online Courses: Creating a Student Workload Calculator. In Proceedings of the 18th European Conference on e-Learning, pp. 629–638. The paper recommends estimating different online learning activities separately and providing time guidance for tasks that cannot be predicted accurately.")


## Text lines are kept to a readable length

Limit ordinary prose to approximately 50–75 characters per line, generally targeting about 60–70 characters. Treat this as a responsive design target rather than a universal optimum: reading performance varies with the content, typeface, device, language and learner.[^line-length-research]

Set the text column with relative units, such as `max-width: 65ch`, and allow it to narrow with the viewport, browser zoom and user-selected font settings. Ensure that learners can achieve lines of no more than 80 characters without horizontal scrolling. Test the result with the supported languages and writing systems rather than assuming that one character measure works equally well for all scripts.[^visual-presentation][^relative-width]

[^line-length-research]: [The influence of reading speed and line length on reading from screen](https://doi.org/10.1006/ijhc.2001.0458 "Dyson, M. C., & Haselgrove, M. (2001). The Influence of Reading Speed and Line Length on the Effectiveness of Reading from Screen. International Journal of Human-Computer Studies, 54(4), 585–612. This study found that a medium line length of 55 characters supported effective reading in the reported experiments, while the broader evidence does not establish one universal optimum.")

[^visual-presentation]: [Understanding visual presentation](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/visual-presentation.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.8: Visual Presentation. This Level AAA criterion supports a mechanism that displays blocks of text at no more than 80 characters or glyphs per line, or 40 for CJK writing systems.")

[^relative-width]: [Using relative measurements for column widths](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/css/C20 "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique C20: Using Relative Measurements to Set Column Widths so that Lines Can Average 80 Characters or Less When the Browser Is Resized. This technique supports using relative units to constrain readable line lengths as the viewport changes.")

## Embedded media may extend beyond the text column when needed

Allow illustrations, diagrams, charts, maps, code examples and embedded media to use more horizontal space than the prose column when their content requires it for legibility or understanding. Determine the width from the information presented rather than applying the same measure to every element.

Keep media responsive, preserve its aspect ratio and prevent it from causing page-level horizontal scrolling. For content that inherently requires a two-dimensional layout, provide a contained scroll area, zoom control or full-screen view that is accessible by keyboard and assistive technologies.[^reflow][^responsive-images]

[^reflow]: [Understanding reflow](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/reflow.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.10: Reflow. This guidance supports content remaining usable without two-dimensional page scrolling at a width equivalent to 320 CSS pixels, except where a two-dimensional layout is essential.")

[^responsive-images]: [Using CSS max-width and height to fit images](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/css/C37.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique C37: Using CSS max-width and height to fit images. This technique supports responsive images that fit the available viewport while preserving their proportions.")


## Hyperlinks do not interrupt the learning activity

Open links to external websites, references and supplementary resources in a separate browser tab so learners can consult them without losing the article, their reading position or the state of the surrounding learning activity. Clearly indicate that the link opens in a new tab. While the standard course flow links should open in the same browser tab.[^new-window-warning]

Use an accessible modal for brief supporting content that learners should review without leaving the activity, such as definitions, source details or short examples. Keep longer or independently navigable resources in a separate tab. Ensure that modals are keyboard-accessible, move focus appropriately, provide a clear close action and return focus to the link that opened them.[^modal-dialog]

[^new-window-warning]: [Giving advance warning when opening a new window](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G201 "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique G201: Giving Users Advanced Warning When Opening a New Window. This technique supports informing users when activating a link will open a new browsing context.")

[^modal-dialog]: [Modal dialog pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/dialog-modal/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: Dialog (Modal) Pattern. This pattern supports focus placement, keyboard interaction, labelling, closing the dialog and returning focus to the triggering element.")


## Reading progress and completion are clearly visualised

Show a progress indicator based on the article’s main content. Clearly distinguish reaching the end of the article from completing any associated learning activities or demonstrating understanding.[^progress-feedback]

Make the current progress available to assistive technologies and do not communicate it through colour alone.[^progressbar][^use-of-colour]

[^progress-feedback]: [The impact of progress indicators on task completion](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intcom.2010.03.001 "Conrad, F. G., Couper, M. P., Tourangeau, R., & Peytchev, A. (2010). The Impact of Progress Indicators on Task Completion. Interacting with Computers, 22(5), 417–427. This study examines how progress indicators affect task completion, supporting the use of article-reading progress feedback.")

[^progressbar]: [WAI-ARIA progressbar role](https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria-1.2/#progressbar "World Wide Web Consortium. (2023). Accessible Rich Internet Applications (WAI-ARIA) 1.2: Progressbar Role. This specification defines semantics that make a progress indicator programmatically available to assistive technologies.")


## Typography supports comfortable and structured reading

Use a screen-readable typeface with clear, distinguishable letterforms. Set ordinary article text at approximately 18–20px on larger screens and never below 16px on smaller screens. Use regular or medium weight for body text, and avoid light, condensed, italicised or uppercase styles for extended passages. Set body line height to approximately 1.5–1.65 and paragraph spacing to about 0.75–1em.[^govuk-type][^uswds-type]

Use a consistent responsive scale for headings: approximately 32–40px for the article title, 24–32px for major sections and 20–24px for subsections. Keep heading line height tighter, around 1.15–1.3, use sentence case and place more space before a heading than after it. Preserve a logical semantic hierarchy without skipping levels, and define sizes and spacing with relative units so learners can resize or override the typography without losing content.[^section-headings][^text-adaptation]

[^govuk-type]: [GOV.UK type scale](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/styles/type-scale/ "GOV.UK Design System. (n.d.). Type Scale. This design-system guidance supports a 19px default body size, responsive heading sizes, relative CSS units and tested line heights.")

[^uswds-type]: [U.S. Web Design System typography](https://designsystem.digital.gov/components/typography/ "U.S. Web Design System. (n.d.). Typography. This guidance supports readable typefaces, type scales and line heights, including at least 1.5 line height for extended reading.")

[^section-headings]: [Understanding section headings](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/section-headings.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2025). Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.10: Section Headings. This guidance supports headings that help learners understand and navigate the organisation of longer content.")

[^text-adaptation]: [Understanding text spacing](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/text-spacing.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2025). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.12: Text Spacing. This guidance supports content remaining usable when learners increase line, paragraph, word and letter spacing.")


## Interactive elements serve a clear learning purpose

Use toggled content, cards, sliders and other interactions only when they support retrieval, comparison, manipulation, progressive disclosure or navigation. Do not add interaction merely to make the article appear more engaging: learner control alone produces little consistent learning benefit, while unnecessary controls can fragment reading and increase extraneous processing.[^learner-control][^coherence-principle]

Keep essential explanations and evidence available without hover, precision dragging or opening every collapsed section. Use established accessible patterns with keyboard and touch operation, visible focus and programmatically exposed labels, values and states. Provide a simpler alternative where an interaction may be difficult to operate, and test whether it improves understanding compared with a static presentation.[^accessible-interactions]

[^learner-control]: [Updated meta-analysis of learner control within educational technology](https://doi.org/10.3102/0034654314526064 "Karich, A. C., Burns, M. K., & Maki, K. E. (2014). Updated Meta-analysis of Learner Control Within Educational Technology. Review of Educational Research, 84(3), 392–410. This meta-analysis found that the overall effect of learner control was close to zero and varied with the instructional context.")

[^accessible-interactions]: [ARIA Authoring Practices Guide](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: Patterns. This guidance supports disclosures, accordions, sliders and other interactive components with appropriate keyboard behaviour, roles, properties and states.")


## Code snippets use appropriate syntax highlighting

Present code as selectable text, preserve meaningful indentation and line breaks, and identify the programming language. Apply consistent syntax highlighting to support scanning, but do not rely on colour alone to explain code structure or meaning; evidence that highlighting improves comprehension is mixed.[^syntax-highlighting][^use-of-colour]

Ensure sufficient contrast, use a legible monospace typeface and provide a clearly labelled copy control for longer examples. Allow code to wrap where this does not alter meaning; otherwise contain horizontal scrolling within the code block rather than the page.[^contrast-minimum][^reflow]

[^syntax-highlighting]: [Does syntax highlighting help programming novices?](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-017-9579-0 "Hannebauer, C., Hesenius, M., & Gruhn, V. (2018). Does Syntax Highlighting Help Programming Novices? Empirical Software Engineering, 23, 2795–2828. This controlled experiment with 390 students found no evidence that highlighting improved correctness on the tested comprehension tasks.")

[^use-of-colour]: [Understanding use of colour](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/use-of-color.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2025). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.1: Use of Color. This guidance supports ensuring that colour is not the only means of conveying information.")

## An audio version of the article is available

Provide an optional, complete [audio][listen-to-audio] version for learners who prefer listening or cannot comfortably read extended text. Keep it synchronised with the current article and provide accessible controls for play, pause, seeking, volume and playback speed. Never start it automatically.[^audio-control]

Always review AI-generated audio before publication. Check pronunciation, clarity, pacing, emphasis, pauses, volume consistency, missing or repeated text and generation artefacts, and confirm that the recording is ready for learners.

Allow learners to read, listen or combine both according to their needs. Do not require simultaneous reading and listening, as identical spoken and written content can create unnecessary processing demands for some learners.[^redundancy]

[^audio-control]: [Understanding audio control](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/audio-control.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2025). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.2: Audio Control. This guidance supports making automatically playing audio avoidable or independently controllable.")

[^redundancy]: [When redundant on-screen text can interfere with learning](https://doi.org/10.1518/hfes.46.3.567.50405 "Kalyuga, S., Chandler, P., & Sweller, J. (2004). When Redundant On-Screen Text in Multimedia Technical Instruction Can Interfere With Learning. Human Factors, 46(3), 567–581. This study supports making an audio version optional rather than requiring simultaneous reading and listening to identical content.")


## Contrast is sufficient, with an enhanced-contrast version available

Ensure text and essential visual content remain distinguishable in the default presentation. Use contrast ratios of at least 4.5:1 for ordinary text, 3:1 for large text and 3:1 for controls, focus indicators and meaningful graphical elements. Do not use colour alone to communicate meaning.[^contrast-minimum][^non-text-contrast]

Provide an easily accessible enhanced-contrast version where appropriate, targeting at least 7:1 for ordinary text and 4.5:1 for large text. Preserve the article’s content, structure and functionality, remember the learner’s choice and support browser or operating-system contrast settings. The enhanced version should supplement—not replace—an accessible default presentation.[^contrast-enhanced][^contrast-switch]

[^contrast-minimum]: [Understanding contrast minimum](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.3: Contrast (Minimum). This guidance supports contrast ratios of at least 4.5:1 for ordinary text and 3:1 for large text.")

[^non-text-contrast]: [Understanding non-text contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.11: Non-text Contrast. This guidance supports contrast ratios of at least 3:1 for essential controls, states and graphical objects against adjacent colours.")

[^contrast-enhanced]: [Understanding contrast enhanced](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-enhanced.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.6: Contrast (Enhanced). This guidance supports enhanced contrast ratios of at least 7:1 for ordinary text and 4.5:1 for large text.")

[^contrast-switch]: [Providing a control to switch to sufficient contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G174.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique G174: Providing a Control with a Sufficient Contrast Ratio That Allows Users to Switch to a Presentation That Uses Sufficient Contrast. This technique supports optional contrast settings for readers who need them.")


## A focused reading view is available, but not the only option

Use semantic HTML so browser reading modes can identify the article, its headings, sections, figures and captions. Where an in-platform reading view is provided, make it optional and easy to leave, and allow learners to adjust text size, spacing and line length.[^reader-mode][^focus-mode]

Preserve links, references, glossary access and essential learning interactions. Do not make the focused view the only accessible version or remove information required to understand or complete the activity.

[^reader-mode]: [The main element](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/main "MDN Web Docs. (n.d.). The main element. This reference supports using main, heading and sectioning elements that browser reader modes use to create a specialised reading view.")

[^focus-mode]: [Why Zen Mode Isn’t the Answer to Everything](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/zen-mode/ "Nielsen Norman Group. (n.d.). Why Zen Mode Isn’t the Answer to Everything. This article supports optional focus modes that reduce visual distractions while preserving necessary orientation and controls.")


## Scrolling and text navigation works with mouse, keyboard and touch

Allow learners to scroll using standard mouse, touch and keyboard controls without custom gestures, scroll hijacking or keyboard traps. Scrollable regions must be reachable, operable and escapable by keyboard, with visible focus that is not obscured.[^keyboard][^input-modalities]

Use semantic headings, landmarks, skip links and a table of contents where appropriate so learners can move directly between sections instead of scrolling through the entire article.[^bypass-blocks][^section-headings]

[^keyboard]: [Understanding keyboard accessibility](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/keyboard.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.1.1: Keyboard. This guidance supports all functionality being operable through a keyboard interface, including access to scrollable content.")

[^input-modalities]: [Understanding input modalities](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/input-modalities.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Guideline 2.5: Input Modalities. This guidance supports the different input methods learners may use, including mouse, touch, keyboard and speech.")

[^bypass-blocks]: [Understanding bypass blocks](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/bypass-blocks.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.1: Bypass Blocks. This guidance supports a mechanism that lets users bypass repeated content and reach the main article.")

## Reading can resume from where the learner stopped

Save the learner’s latest meaningful reading position and offer to continue from it when they return, while preserving the option to restart from the beginning.[^task-resumption]

Restore the nearest section, paragraph or content anchor rather than only a pixel-based scroll position, which may become inaccurate when the viewport or article layout changes. Synchronise the position across signed-in devices where appropriate, and reset or migrate it when the article is substantially revised.[^scroll-restoration]

[^task-resumption]: [Interruption, recall and resumption](https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-025-05000-w "Ghibellini, R., & Meier, B. (2025). Interruption, Recall and Resumption: A Meta-analysis of the Zeigarnik and Ovsiankina Effects. Humanities and Social Sciences Communications, 12, Article 962. This meta-analysis found that participants often resumed interrupted tasks when given another opportunity.")

[^scroll-restoration]: [History scroll restoration](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/History/scrollRestoration "MDN Web Docs. (n.d.). History: scrollRestoration property. This reference supports restoring or manually controlling the document’s scroll position when navigating browser history.")

## Text can be selected, bookmarked and annotated

Allow learners to select and copy article text using standard mouse, keyboard and touch interactions. Provide an optional way to bookmark a passage and attach a private note, supporting active reading, later review and connections between ideas.

Anchor each bookmark to the selected text and its surrounding context rather than only a page position. Preserve bookmarks and notes across sessions and devices where appropriate, and provide controls to edit, delete and export them. Ensure annotations remain recoverable or are clearly marked when the article changes.[^web-annotation]

[^web-annotation]: [Web Annotation Data Model](https://www.w3.org/TR/annotation-model/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2017). Web Annotation Data Model. This standard supports associating comments, notes and other annotations with complete resources or selected passages and reusing them across systems.")

## Read articles are clearly distinguished

Mark articles that the learner has completed with a persistent visual status, such as a **Read** label, checkmark or progress indicator. Keep the article title and other identifying information recognisable, and do not rely on colour alone to communicate the status.[^use-of-colour]

Expose the read state programmatically so it can be identified by assistive technologies. Treat it as a record of reading activity—not evidence that the learner understood or mastered the content—and allow learners to mark the article as unread where appropriate.[^status-messages]

[^status-messages]: [Understanding status messages](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/status-messages.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 4.1.3: Status Messages. This guidance supports making status information programmatically available to assistive technologies.")

## All text interactions are tested on mobile and touch screens

Test links, footnotes, glossary terms, disclosures, copy controls, text selection and other article interactions on supported mobile devices in portrait and landscape orientations. Ensure they work by touch without relying on hover, precise gestures or desktop-specific controls.[^pointer-gestures]

Keep touch targets sufficiently large and separated, and ensure interactive content does not obstruct reading, browser zoom or text selection. Verify that the article reflows without page-level horizontal scrolling and that the same information and functionality remain available on smaller screens.[^target-size][^reflow]

[^pointer-gestures]: [Understanding pointer gestures](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/pointer-gestures.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.5.1: Pointer Gestures. This guidance supports avoiding dependence on complex or multipoint gestures when a simpler pointer action can be provided.")

[^target-size]: [Understanding target size](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.5.8: Target Size (Minimum). This guidance supports interactive targets being sufficiently large or separated for reliable operation.")

## The next learning activity is clearly emphasised after article completion

After the learner reaches the end of the article or marks it as complete, present a prominent next action, such as reading the next article, reviewing key takeaways, opening support materials or completing a retrieval, reflection or application activity.

Keep the action accessible and relevant to the learning sequence. Preserve access to the completed article and do not automatically move learners away before they can review important passages, references or notes.[^focus-order]

[^focus-order]: [Understanding focus order](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-order.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.3: Focus Order. This guidance supports focus moving in an order that preserves meaning and operability.")


{% /pattern-list %}

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="system" %}
# System design

## Reading completion and analytics are defined and proportionate

Define what completion means, such as learner-declared completion or submission of required activities, and distinguish it from inferred behaviours such as reaching the end, scroll depth or time spent. Treat completion as participation data—not evidence of attention, understanding or mastery.[^analytics-limits]

Collect only the minimum analytics needed to answer a defined educational, content-quality or usability question. Do not enable heat maps, cursor tracking or detailed behavioural recording by default; use them only where a justified research question, appropriate learner choice and clear governance exist. Explain the purpose, access and retention of collected data, aggregate it where possible, and never use behavioural analytics alone to grade learners or infer disengagement.[^learning-analytics-ethics][^analytics-privacy]

[^analytics-limits]: [Understanding privacy and data protection issues in learning analytics](https://doi.org/10.1111/bjet.13388 "Liu, Q., & Khalil, M. (2023). Understanding Privacy and Data Protection Issues in Learning Analytics Using a Systematic Review. British Journal of Educational Technology, 54(6), 1715–1747. This systematic review identifies privacy and data-protection issues relevant to limiting and governing learning analytics.")

[^learning-analytics-ethics]: [Code of practice for learning analytics](https://www.jisc.ac.uk/guides/code-of-practice-for-learning-analytics "Jisc. (2015). Code of Practice for Learning Analytics. This code of practice supports responsibility, transparency, consent, privacy, access, data validity and learner safeguards.")

[^analytics-privacy]: [Principles of the GDPR](https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/principles-gdpr_en "European Commission. (n.d.). Principles of the General Data Protection Regulation. This guidance explains purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation and security, relevant to defining and governing learning analytics.")


## Typos and mistakes can be reported

Provide a clearly labelled **Report a typo or mistake** action without requiring learners to leave the article. When text is selected, include the selected passage, article section and content version automatically, and allow the learner to add a brief explanation or suggested correction. Collect only the information needed to investigate the report.[^page-feedback]

Where a keyboard shortcut is provided, use `Ctrl` + `Enter` on Windows and Linux and `Command` + `Enter` on macOS as a consistent product convention. Activate it only when article text is selected or the reporting interface has focus, display the shortcut beside the action and keep the visible action available as the primary method.[^keyboard-access]

Confirm that the report was received while preserving the learner’s reading position. Keep entered information when validation fails, describe errors clearly and make confirmation messages available to assistive technologies. Route reports into a defined editorial workflow so they can be reviewed, corrected and closed.[^form-notifications][^status-messages]

[^page-feedback]: [Community](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/community/ "GOV.UK Design System. (n.d.). Community. This guidance supports a clearly identified way for users to suggest improvements, report bugs and correct errors.")

[^keyboard-access]: [Developing a keyboard interface](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/practices/keyboard-interface/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: Developing a Keyboard Interface. This guidance supports predictable keyboard operation and avoiding conflicts between keyboard shortcuts, assistive technologies, browsers and operating systems.")

[^form-notifications]: [User notifications in forms](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/forms/notifications/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2022). Forms Tutorial: User Notifications. This guidance supports accessible validation errors, submission feedback and association of messages with form controls.")

## Default system typography supports target locales and character sets

Use a Unicode-compatible system font stack with explicit fallback fonts for every target script. Verify that body text, headings, controls and emphasis styles contain the required letters, diacritics, combining marks, punctuation, numerals and symbols without missing glyphs or inappropriate substitutions. Do not assume that one typeface supports every target language.[^css-fonts][^unicode-rendering]

Test typography with representative localized content, including mixed scripts, bold and italic styles, contextual shaping, locale-specific letterforms and line breaking. Choose fallbacks with compatible proportions and an appropriate typographic style, and replace or supplement the default system stack where it cannot render a target locale correctly.[^font-fallback][^language-enablement]

[^css-fonts]: [CSS Fonts Module Level 4](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-fonts-4/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). CSS Fonts Module Level 4. This specification explains font selection across declared families and characters, supporting deliberate fallback configuration.")

[^unicode-rendering]: [Combining marks](https://www.unicode.org/versions/Unicode17.0.0/core-spec/chapter-7/ "The Unicode Consortium. (n.d.). The Unicode Standard, Version 17.0, Chapter 7. This specification supports fonts and shaping systems correctly positioning combining marks and diacritics with their base characters.")

[^font-fallback]: [Typeface styles and font fallback](https://www.w3.org/International/articles/typography/fontstyles.en.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2025). Typeface Styles and Font Fallback. This guidance supports fallback fonts that preserve an appropriate style and account for language-specific glyph forms.")

[^language-enablement]: [Language Enablement Index](https://www.w3.org/TR/typography/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2024). Language Enablement Index. This index supports recognising that writing systems have distinct requirements for fonts, letterforms, shaping, line breaking, directionality and other aspects of typography.")

## Content language is correctly identified in HTML

Set the primary language of the article page with a valid BCP 47 `lang` attribute on the root `<html>` element. This enables browsers, screen readers and other assistive technologies to apply the appropriate pronunciation, writing direction, hyphenation and language rules.[^page-language]

When a passage, quotation, glossary term or interactive element uses a different language, set `lang` on the nearest containing element. Do not mark proper names, technical terms or commonly adopted foreign words unless identifying the language is necessary for correct pronunciation or interpretation.[^language-parts]

[^page-language]: [Using the language attribute on the HTML element](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/html/H57.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique H57: Using the lang Attribute on the HTML Element. This technique supports declaring the page’s default language so it is available to browsers and assistive technologies.")

[^language-parts]: [Using language attributes to identify language changes](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/html/H58.html "World Wide Web Consortium. (2025). Technique H58: Using Language Attributes to Identify Changes in the Human Language. This technique supports marking passages that switch to another human language.")

## Scrolling and animated behaviours work across all target platforms

Test scrolling, anchor navigation, sticky elements, transitions and other animated behaviours across supported browsers, operating systems, devices and input methods. Preserve the same content, state and functionality when smooth scrolling or animation is unsupported, interrupted or implemented differently by the user agent.[^scroll-behaviour]

Avoid scroll hijacking, parallax effects or animations that interfere with reading and navigation. Respect the learner’s operating-system motion preference by reducing or removing non-essential smooth scrolling, transitions and movement when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` is active.[^reduced-motion]

[^scroll-behaviour]: [CSS Overflow Module Level 3](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-overflow-3/ "World Wide Web Consortium. (2025). CSS Overflow Module Level 3. This specification explains that user agents determine the timing of smooth scrolling and may ignore the scroll-behavior property.")

[^reduced-motion]: [Using prefers-reduced-motion to prevent motion](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/css/C39 "World Wide Web Consortium. (2026). Technique C39: Using the CSS prefers-reduced-motion Query to Prevent Motion. This technique supports non-essential motion triggered by interaction respecting the user’s system preference.")

{% /pattern-list %}

